eaxmple and explain about the Sequence length and Padding

Sequence length = the maximum number of tokens allowed in one sequence.


Why do we use Padding?
In a Seq2Seq model, different sentences can have different lengths.

For example:
English sentence 1 → "I am Mani"
English sentence 2 → "I am learning machine learning"

After tokenization:
"I am Mani"
→ [5, 8, 12]

"I am learning machine learning"
→ [5, 8, 20, 15, 20]

Their lengths are different:
3 tokens
5 tokens

But neural networks usually process data in batches, so the sequences in a batch need to have the same length.

That's why we use padding.


What is Padding?
Padding means adding a special value, usually 0, to shorter sequences so that all sequences have the same length.

For example, if our maximum length is 5:
[5, 8, 12]

becomes:
[5, 8, 12, 0, 0]

And:
[5, 8, 20, 15, 20]

stays:
[5, 8, 20, 15, 20]

Now both have length 5.

Exactly — if the sentence has 25 tokens and maxlen=20, what happens to the remaining 5?
They are removed (truncated).

Why does this happen?
Because you told pad_sequences():
"I only want a maximum of 20 tokens."


Simple :
Less than 20 → add 0s
Exactly 20    → no change
More than 20 → remove extra tokens

What is seq_length?
Sequence length = the maximum number of tokens allowed in one sequence.


Simple notes:
Padding:
Adds zeros to shorter sequences so that all sequences have the same length.

Sequence length:
Defines the maximum number of tokens allowed in a sequence.

maxlen=20:
Every sequence will have 20 positions; shorter sequences are padded and longer ones are truncated.

padding='post':
Adds padding zeros at the end of the sequence.

One more important concept: in Seq2Seq, those 0s are normally treated as padding/masked positions, so the model doesn't learn that 0 is an actual word.


